theory.intro:
On the unit circle, $\tan\theta$ is the **gradient** of the radius $OP$. Gradient is rise over run, $\dfrac{y}{x}$, and since $x=\cos\theta$ and $y=\sin\theta$ this gives $\tan\theta=\dfrac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (X) at (1.2,0);
\coordinate (P) at (62:2.4);
\coordinate (F) at ({2.4*cos(62)},0);
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (O) circle (2.4);
\draw[thick] (O)--(P);
\draw[red, dashed] (F)--(P) node[midway, right] {$\sin\theta$};
\draw[blue, dashed] (O)--(F) node[midway, below=3pt] {$\cos\theta$};
\fill (P) circle (1.6pt);
\node[above right] at (P) {$P$};
\node[below left] at (O) {$O$};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.75cm, angle eccentricity=1.3] {angle=X--O--P};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

